CHUNK({"chunk":"2a","module":"m2","topics":[{"id":"m2-semiconductors","title":"Semiconductors and doping","short":"Semiconductors, doping","blurb":"Why pure silicon is nearly an insulator, how a pinch of impurity turns it into N-type or P-type material, and who carries the current in each.","source":"Pages 1–10","tabs":[{"title":"Conductor, insulator, semiconductor","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Materials split by how easily their <b>valence electrons</b> move. Semiconductors sit in between, and we can tune them.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Valence electrons</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Conductor</td><td>Usually metals; electrons travel easily when an emf (voltage) is applied</td><td>Copper: 29 protons, 29 electrons, and its valence electron hops from atom to atom</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insulator</td><td>Held firmly to the nucleus</td><td>High resistance, no current flows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Semiconductor</td><td>Between the two</td><td>Relatively good conductor and relatively good insulator, depending on the conditions applied</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>The energy band diagram in the course material shows the same story: in a conductor the bands touch, in an insulator there is a wide gap, in a semiconductor the gap is small.</p></div>"},{"title":"Intrinsic silicon","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Silicon has <b>14 protons and 14 electrons</b>. Its atoms link together into a <b>crystal lattice</b> through <b>covalent bonds</b>.</p>\n<p>In pure silicon every valence electron is tied up in a bond, so no electrons are free for current. Pure semiconductor material is called <b>intrinsic</b>, and silicon in this form is a <b>great insulator</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>Each atom shares its outer electrons with its neighbours, so the electrons are difficult to dislodge. No free carriers means no current.</p></div>"},{"title":"Doping, N-type and P-type","html":"<p class=\"lead\">To make a semiconductor conduct, other atoms called <b>impurities</b> are added. This is <b>doping</b>, and the result is an <b>extrinsic semiconductor</b> with good conductivity.</p>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>N-type</h4><p>Dope with <b>arsenic</b> (5 valence electrons). Four bond with neighbouring silicon atoms and the <b>one left-over electron</b> per arsenic atom is free to carry current. Silicon doped with extra electrons.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>P-type</h4><p>Dope with <b>boron</b> (3 valence electrons). Its three bonds are fine but one electron is missing from the fourth bond. That empty place is a <b>hole</b>, which acts as a positive charge and attracts electrons. Silicon doped with material missing electrons.</p></div></div>\n<p><b>Holes are a second type of current carrier</b>, like electrons.</p>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>More dopant atoms means more free carriers and lower resistance; fewer dopant atoms means higher resistance. By controlling the amount of doping, virtually any resistance can be achieved.</p></div>"},{"title":"Current in N and P material","html":"<p class=\"lead\">In both types the supply pushes current from its positive to its negative terminal, but different carriers do the work inside.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>N-type</th><th>P-type</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Majority carriers</td><td><b>Electrons</b></td><td><b>Holes</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minority carriers</td><td>Holes</td><td>Electrons</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What happens</td><td>The positive terminal pulls free electrons away from their atoms. Electrons from the negative terminal enter and are attracted to the positively charged atoms.</td><td>Electrons from the negative terminal fill the holes. The positive terminal pulls electrons out of holes, so those holes attract more electrons. Inside the material the current is the movement of holes from positive to negative.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p>Write majority and minority carriers for both types. It is the most asked line from this topic.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is pure (intrinsic) silicon a good insulator?","back":"All its valence electrons are locked in covalent bonds in the crystal lattice, so there are no free carriers."},{"front":"What is doping, and what is the doped material called?","back":"Adding impurity atoms to a pure semiconductor so it conducts. The result is an extrinsic semiconductor."},{"front":"Majority and minority carriers in N-type and in P-type?","back":"N-type: electrons are majority, holes minority. P-type: holes are majority, electrons minority."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-pn-junction","title":"PN junction and biasing","short":"PN junction, bias","blurb":"Join a P half and an N half and a barrier forms. Forward bias removes it, reverse bias widens it, and a big enough reverse voltage breaks the junction down.","source":"Pages 11–16","tabs":[{"title":"Forming the junction","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Dope one half of a semiconductor <b>P-type</b> and the other half <b>N-type</b> and you get a <b>PN junction</b>. The plane dividing the two halves is the junction.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Diffusion</b> N-type has many free electrons and P-type has many holes, so at the junction electrons diffuse to the P-side and holes diffuse to the N-side.</li><li><b>Charge builds</b> The net opposite charge left in each layer stops further diffusion into that layer.</li><li><b>Barrier</b> A barrier forms near the junction and blocks further movement of carriers. This is the <b>potential barrier</b>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Barrier, germanium</span><b>0.3 V</b></div><div><span>Barrier, silicon</span><b>0.7 V</b></div></div>\n<p>A diode can work in three conditions: <b>no bias</b>, <b>forward bias</b> and <b>reverse bias</b>.</p>"},{"title":"Forward bias","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Connect the battery <b>positive to P</b> and <b>negative to N</b>: forward bias.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-pn-bias\"></figure>\n<ul><li>The applied positive potential repels holes in the P region towards the junction, and the negative potential repels electrons in the N region towards the junction.</li><li>While the applied voltage V<sub>F</sub> is <b>less than the internal barrier V<sub>0</sub></b>, the forward current I<sub>F</sub> is almost zero.</li><li>Once the applied potential is <b>more than V<sub>0</sub></b> the barrier disappears. Holes cross from P to N and electrons cross the other way, giving a <b>relatively large current</b> in the external circuit.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Reverse bias","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Connect the battery <b>negative to P</b> and <b>positive to N</b>: reverse bias.</p>\n<ul><li>Holes in the P side move towards the battery’s negative terminal and electrons in the N side move towards the positive terminal.</li><li>The <b>depletion region</b>, which has no mobile carriers, becomes <b>wider</b>.</li><li>The field from the applied voltage points the same way as the junction’s own field, so the barrier increases.</li><li>In theory no current flows. In practice a very small <b>reverse saturation current</b>, in the order of <b>microamperes</b>, flows.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>The reverse saturation current depends mainly on <b>junction temperature</b>, because its source is minority carriers made by thermally broken covalent bonds.</p></div>"},{"title":"Breakdown","html":"<p class=\"lead\">If the reverse bias is made large, the junction <b>breaks down</b> and a very large reverse current flows.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Acceleration</b> Free electrons from the N-type, moving towards the positive terminal, gain enough energy to move at high velocity.</li><li><b>Knock-out</b> They dislodge valence electrons from semiconductor atoms in the crystal.</li><li><b>Avalanche</b> A large number of free electrons forms, called an avalanche of free electrons.</li></ol>\n<p>The reverse voltage at which this happens is the <b>breakdown voltage</b>.</p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the potential barrier of a silicon diode and of a germanium diode?","back":"Silicon 0.7 V, germanium 0.3 V."},{"front":"How does the potential barrier form?","back":"Electrons diffuse to the P-side and holes to the N-side. The net opposite charge left in each layer stops further diffusion, setting up the barrier."},{"front":"What is forward bias?","back":"Battery positive to P-type, negative to N-type. Above V0 the barrier vanishes and large current flows."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-diode-vi","title":"Diode V–I characteristics and rectifiers","short":"Diode V–I, rectifiers","blurb":"The shape of the diode’s voltage–current curve, and the job it is most used for: turning AC into DC.","source":"Pages 17–19","tabs":[{"title":"V–I characteristics","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Plot the current through a diode against the voltage across it. The curve has a forward side and a reverse side.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-diode-vi\"></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Forward:</b> almost no current until the “knee”, then the current rises steeply. The knee is at <b>0.3 V for germanium</b> and <b>0.7 V for silicon</b>.</li><li><b>Reverse:</b> only a tiny leakage current: <b>below 20 µA for silicon</b> and <b>below 50 µA for germanium</b>.</li><li>At the <b>reverse breakdown voltage</b> the reverse current shoots up (the Zener / avalanche region).</li></ul>\n<p>Symbol: the arrow side is the <b>anode (A)</b>, the bar side is the <b>cathode (K)</b>. Conventional current flows from anode to cathode.</p>"},{"title":"Rectifier","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <b>rectifier</b> converts <b>AC</b>, which periodically reverses direction, into <b>DC</b>, which flows in one direction only. The process is <b>rectification</b>.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-rectifiers\"></figure>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Half-wave</h4><p>Either the positive or the negative half of the AC wave is passed and the other half is blocked. Only half of the input reaches the output, so it is <b>very inefficient</b> for power transfer.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Full-wave</h4><p>The whole input waveform is converted to <b>one constant polarity</b> at the output. Both polarities become DC, so it is <b>more efficient</b>. The figure uses four diodes D<sub>1</sub> to D<sub>4</sub> (a bridge).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p>Draw the circuit, then the input and output waveforms one above the other. The half-wave output has gaps where the blocked half-cycle was; the full-wave output has none.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where is the knee voltage on a silicon and a germanium diode curve?","back":"Silicon 0.7 V, germanium 0.3 V."},{"front":"How big is the reverse leakage current in silicon and germanium diodes?","back":"Less than 20 µA in silicon and less than 50 µA in germanium."},{"front":"What is a rectifier?","back":"A device that converts AC to DC (one direction only). The process is rectification."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-zener","title":"Zener diode","short":"Zener diode","blurb":"A diode built to work in reverse breakdown, where its voltage stays almost constant. That makes it a voltage regulator.","source":"Pages 20–24","tabs":[{"title":"What it is","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <b>Zener diode</b> is a silicon PN junction device operated in its <b>reverse breakdown region</b>.</p>\n<p>In an ordinary diode, breakdown means high current and high power at the junction. That is destructive and the diode is damaged. A Zener is <b>heavily doped</b> and designed with enough power-dissipation capability to run safely in breakdown.</p>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Zener potentials</span><b>1.8 V to 200 V</b></div><div><span>Power ratings</span><b>¼ W to 50 W</b></div><div><span>Material</span><b>Silicon</b></div></div>\n<p>Silicon is preferred because it handles higher temperature and current.</p>"},{"title":"V–I curve and breakdown","html":"<p class=\"lead\">In forward bias a Zener behaves like an ordinary diode. In reverse bias it <b>breaks down</b>, and the breakdown voltage depends on the <b>amount of doping</b>.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-zener-vi\"></figure>\n<ul><li>Heavy doping means a <b>thin depletion layer</b>, so breakdown comes at a <b>lower reverse voltage</b> and is <b>sharp</b>. You choose the breakdown voltage through the doping.</li><li>A strong reverse field tears bound electrons out of covalent bonds (direct rupture), creating many electron–hole pairs and a large reverse current at the breakdown voltage.</li><li>In the breakdown region the voltage stays almost <b>constant (V<sub>Z</sub>)</b> while the current moves between <b>I<sub>Z(min)</sub></b> and <b>I<sub>Z(max)</sub></b>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"note trap\"><p>Zener breakdown occurs at lower breakdown voltages and avalanche breakdown at higher ones, but such diodes are normally all called Zener diodes.</p></div>"},{"title":"Voltage regulator","html":"<p class=\"lead\">In reverse breakdown the diode voltage stays almost constant even as its current rises, so it serves as a <b>reference voltage</b> and can be used as a <b>voltage regulator</b>.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-zener-reg\"></figure>\n<ul><li>Use it when you need a <b>constant voltage across a load R<sub>L</sub></b> while the input voltage varies over a range.</li><li>The Zener is <b>reverse biased</b>, in parallel with the load, with a series resistor R from the input V<sub>i</sub>.</li><li>As long as V<sub>i</sub> does <b>not fall below V<sub>Z</sub></b>, the voltage across the diode is constant, so the load voltage V<sub>o</sub> is constant too.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Applications","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Diodes in general, and Zeners in particular, appear in these circuits.</p>\n<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Diode circuits</h4><ul><li>Rectifiers: AC to DC for DC-operated circuits</li><li>Battery charging circuits</li><li>Protection against overcurrent, polarity reversal, and currents from an inductive kick in a relay circuit</li></ul></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Zener circuits</h4><ul><li>Overvoltage protection</li><li>Setting reference voltages</li></ul></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a Zener diode?","back":"A heavily doped silicon PN junction diode operated in the reverse breakdown region."},{"front":"Why does an ordinary diode get damaged in breakdown but a Zener does not?","back":"The Zener is designed with adequate power dissipation to operate safely in breakdown."},{"front":"How does doping set the Zener breakdown voltage?","back":"Heavier doping gives a thinner depletion layer, so breakdown is at a lower reverse voltage and is sharper."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-bjt-basics","title":"BJT structure and biasing","short":"BJT structure, bias","blurb":"The three-terminal transistor: its three regions, the two symbols, and the bias voltages that make it work.","source":"Pages 26–33","tabs":[{"title":"What a BJT is","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <b>Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT)</b> is a <b>three-terminal</b> semiconductor device whose operation depends on both <b>majority and minority carriers</b>, hence “bipolar”. It uses both electrons and holes. Unipolar transistors, such as FETs, use only one kind of carrier.</p>\n<ul><li>It is used as a <b>linear amplifier</b> to boost a signal and as an <b>electronic switch</b>.</li><li>It is a PN junction diode with a <b>second P or N region</b> added.</li><li><b>NPN</b>: two N regions and one P region. <b>PNP</b>: two P regions and one N region. Either way there are two junctions.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Terminals</span><b>Collector (C), Emitter (E), Base (B)</b></div><div><span>Junctions</span><b>Collector–base (CBJ), base–emitter (BEJ)</b></div></div>"},{"title":"Emitter, base, collector","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The three regions differ in doping and size on purpose.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Doping and size</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Emitter</td><td><b>Heavily doped</b>, more than the other regions</td><td>Supply majority charge carriers (electrons or holes) to the base</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Base</td><td>Middle section, <b>very thin</b> and <b>very lightly doped</b></td><td>Pass most carriers on to the collector</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collector</td><td>Physically <b>larger</b> than the emitter</td><td>Collect the carriers that come from the emitter through the base</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>The collector is made larger because it must dissipate much greater power. Because of this difference you cannot swap emitter and collector: the transistor cannot be inverted.</p></div>"},{"title":"Symbols","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The <b>arrowhead is always on the emitter</b>, never on the collector, and it shows the direction of conventional current flow.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-bjt-symbols\"></figure>\n<ul><li><b>PNP:</b> arrowhead points from emitter to base. The emitter is positive with respect to the base (and the collector).</li><li><b>NPN:</b> arrowhead points from base to emitter. The base (and the collector) is positive with respect to the emitter.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Biasing and currents","html":"<p class=\"lead\">For proper working you apply voltages of the correct polarity across both junctions, the collector–base and emitter–base junctions. This is <b>biasing</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p><b>Active region:</b> the emitter–base junction is <b>forward biased</b> and the collector–base junction is <b>reverse biased</b>.</p></div>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-bjt-currents\"></figure>\n<p>Three primary currents flow in a properly biased transistor: I<sub>E</sub>, I<sub>B</sub> and I<sub>C</sub>.</p>\n<div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"Both NPN and PNP\">I<sub>E</sub> = I<sub>B</sub> + I<sub>C</sub></div>\n<p>A small part (about <b>1–2 %</b>) of the emitter current becomes the base current, and the remaining <b>98–99 %</b> becomes the collector current.</p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is the BJT called bipolar?","back":"Its operation depends on both majority and minority carriers (electrons and holes)."},{"front":"Which BJT region is heavily doped, and why?","back":"The emitter, because it must supply majority carriers to the base."},{"front":"Describe the base of a BJT.","back":"The middle section, very thin and very lightly doped."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":1},{"id":"m2-bjt-operation","title":"Working of NPN and PNP","short":"NPN and PNP working","blurb":"How carriers move through a properly biased transistor and why only a small base current is needed.","source":"Pages 34–37","tabs":[{"title":"NPN transistor","html":"<p class=\"lead\">In an NPN transistor the emitter pushes <b>electrons</b> into the base, and the collector sweeps them up.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Injection</b> The forward bias on the emitter–base junction makes a lot of electrons cross from the emitter into the base.</li><li><b>Few recombine</b> The base is lightly doped P-type, so it has very few holes. Only a few electrons combine with holes there, and that makes the small <b>base current I<sub>B</sub></b>.</li><li><b>Collection</b> The reverse-biased collector junction has a strong electric field that sweeps most of the injected electrons (almost <b>95 %</b>) into the collector, forming the <b>collector current I<sub>C</sub></b>.</li><li><b>Sum</b> The base and collector currents add to give the emitter current: I<sub>E</sub> = I<sub>B</sub> + I<sub>C</sub>.</li></ol>"},{"title":"PNP transistor","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A PNP transistor works the same way with <b>holes</b> as the main carriers.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Injection</b> The forward bias on the emitter–base junction makes many holes cross from the emitter into the base.</li><li><b>Few recombine</b> The base is lightly doped N-type, so it has very few electrons. Only a few holes combine with electrons there, giving the small <b>base current I<sub>B</sub></b>.</li><li><b>Collection</b> The remaining holes (more than <b>95 %</b>) cross into the collector, forming the <b>collector current I<sub>C</sub></b>.</li><li><b>Sum</b> I<sub>E</sub> = I<sub>B</sub> + I<sub>C</sub>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>The thin, lightly doped base is the whole trick. Carriers have almost nowhere to recombine, so nearly all of them reach the collector.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is the base current so small in an NPN transistor?","back":"The base is thin and lightly doped P-type with few holes, so very few injected electrons recombine there."},{"front":"What sweeps electrons from base to collector in an NPN?","back":"The strong electric field of the reverse-biased collector junction. Almost 95 % get through."},{"front":"Which carriers carry the main current in a PNP?","back":"Holes, injected from the emitter. More than 95 % cross into the collector."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-bjt-config","title":"Configurations and CE characteristics","short":"CB, CE, CC and curves","blurb":"The three ways to wire a transistor, and the input and output curves of the common-emitter circuit with its three regions.","source":"Pages 38–42","tabs":[{"title":"The three configurations","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A transistor has three terminals, so one terminal has to be <b>common</b> to the input and output circuits. That gives three configurations.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-bjt-config\"></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Common-base (CB)</span><span class=\"chip\">Common-emitter (CE)</span><span class=\"chip\">Common-collector (CC)</span></div>\n<p>“Common” names the electrode shared by the input and output. Since the common electrode is usually grounded, these are also called <b>grounded-base</b>, <b>grounded-emitter</b> and <b>grounded-collector</b> configurations.</p>\n<div class=\"note exam\"><p><b>CE</b> gives high voltage gain, current gain and power gain compared with the other configurations.</p></div>"},{"title":"CE input characteristic","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The input characteristic shows how <b>I<sub>B</sub> varies with V<sub>BE</sub></b> when <b>V<sub>CE</sub> is held constant</b>.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-ce-input\"></figure>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Fix V<sub>CE</sub></b> Keep V<sub>CE</sub> at a convenient constant value.</li><li><b>Sweep V<sub>BE</sub></b> Increase V<sub>BE</sub> in steps and note I<sub>B</sub> at each step.</li><li><b>Repeat</b> Do it again for a different constant V<sub>CE</sub>. This gives a family of curves (V<sub>CE</sub> = 0 V, 10 V, 20 V in the figure).</li></ol>\n<p>I<sub>B</sub> stays near zero for low V<sub>BE</sub> and then climbs steeply, like a forward-biased diode.</p>"},{"title":"CE output characteristic","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The output characteristic shows how <b>I<sub>C</sub> varies with V<sub>CE</sub></b> when <b>I<sub>B</sub> is held constant</b>.</p>\n<figure data-fig=\"m2-ce-output\"></figure>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Fix I<sub>B</sub></b> Set I<sub>B</sub> to a convenient constant value.</li><li><b>Sweep V<sub>CE</sub></b> Increase V<sub>CE</sub> from zero in steps and note I<sub>C</sub> each time.</li><li><b>Repeat</b> Reduce V<sub>CE</sub> to zero, set I<sub>B</sub> to another value and repeat. The result is a family of curves (I<sub>B</sub> = 0 to 60 µA in the figure).</li></ol>"},{"title":"Three regions","html":"<p class=\"lead\">The output curves divide into <b>active</b>, <b>cut-off</b> and <b>saturation</b> regions.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Junctions</th><th>Conditions</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Active</td><td>CBJ reverse biased, BEJ forward biased</td><td>I<sub>C</sub> follows I<sub>B</sub></td><td>Voltage, current or power <b>amplification</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cut-off</td><td>Both off</td><td>I<sub>B</sub> = 0, I<sub>C</sub> = 0, V<sub>CE</sub> maximum; large depletion layer, no current</td><td>Switch <b>fully OFF</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Saturation</td><td>Both on</td><td>Maximum base current, maximum I<sub>C</sub>, minimum V<sub>CE</sub>; depletion layer as small as possible</td><td>Switch <b>fully ON</b></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>A transistor used as a switch jumps between cut-off and saturation. Used as an amplifier it stays in the active region.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the three BJT configurations.","back":"Common-base (CB), common-emitter (CE) and common-collector (CC)."},{"front":"What does “common” mean in a configuration?","back":"The electrode shared by the input and output circuits, usually grounded."},{"front":"Which configuration gives the highest voltage, current and power gain?","back":"Common-emitter (CE)."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-bjt-gains","title":"Alpha, beta and current relations","short":"α, β relations","blurb":"The two current gains of a transistor and the formulas that convert between them and the three currents.","source":"Pages 43–45","tabs":[{"title":"Alpha","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Alpha (α)</b> is the ratio of collector current to emitter current.</p>\n<div class=\"eq\">α = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>C</sub></span><span>I<sub>E</sub></span></span></div>\n<ul><li>α measures the <b>quality</b> of a transistor: the higher it is, the more closely I<sub>C</sub> equals I<sub>E</sub>.</li><li>Its value ranges from <b>0.95 to 0.999</b>.</li><li>α is <b>always less than unity</b>, because I<sub>C</sub> is I<sub>E</sub> minus the small I<sub>B</sub>.</li></ul>\n<p>The load resistance R<sub>L</sub> is connected in series with the collector, and the current gain of the common-emitter configuration is quite large.</p>"},{"title":"Beta and the relations","html":"<p class=\"lead\"><b>Beta (β)</b> is the ratio of collector current to base current.</p>\n<div class=\"eq\">β = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>C</sub></span><span>I<sub>B</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>C</sub></span><span>I<sub>E</sub> − I<sub>C</sub></span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>α</span><span>1 − α</span></span></div>\n<p>It is possible for β to be as high as <b>500</b>. The other direction:</p>\n<div class=\"eq\">α = <span class=\"frac\"><span>β</span><span>1 + β</span></span></div>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>Dividing top and bottom of α/(1 − α) by I<sub>E</sub> shows why: I<sub>E</sub> − I<sub>C</sub> is just I<sub>B</sub>, so α/(1 − α) = I<sub>C</sub>/I<sub>B</sub>.</p></div>"},{"title":"Current relations","html":"<p class=\"lead\">Given any one current and either gain, you can find the others.</p>\n<div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"IC from IB and IE\">I<sub>C</sub> = βI<sub>B</sub> = αI<sub>E</sub> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>β</span><span>1 + β</span></span> I<sub>E</sub></div>\n<div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"IB from IC and IE\">I<sub>B</sub> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>C</sub></span><span>β</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>E</sub></span><span>1 + β</span></span> = (1 − α) I<sub>E</sub></div>\n<div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"IE from IC and IB\">I<sub>E</sub> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>C</sub></span><span>α</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1 + β</span><span>β</span></span> I<sub>C</sub> = (1 + β) I<sub>B</sub> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>I<sub>B</sub></span><span>1 − α</span></span></div>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A transistor has I<sub>B</sub> = 50 µA and β = 100. Find I<sub>C</sub>, I<sub>E</sub> and α.</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">I<sub>C</sub> = βI<sub>B</sub> = 100 × 50 µA = 5 mA</div><div class=\"eq\">I<sub>E</sub> = (1 + β)I<sub>B</sub> = 101 × 50 µA = 5.05 mA</div><div class=\"eq\">α = β/(1 + β) = 100/101</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>I<sub>C</sub> = 5 mA, I<sub>E</sub> = 5.05 mA, α = 0.990</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A transistor has α = 0.98 and I<sub>E</sub> = 10 mA. Find β, I<sub>C</sub> and I<sub>B</sub>.</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">β = α/(1 − α) = 0.98/0.02 = 49</div><div class=\"eq\">I<sub>C</sub> = αI<sub>E</sub> = 0.98 × 10 mA = 9.8 mA</div><div class=\"eq\">I<sub>B</sub> = (1 − α)I<sub>E</sub> = 0.02 × 10 mA = 0.2 mA</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>β = 49, I<sub>C</sub> = 9.8 mA, I<sub>B</sub> = 0.2 mA</p></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Define alpha of a transistor and its range.","back":"α = I_C / I_E. It lies between 0.95 and 0.999 and is always less than 1."},{"front":"Define beta of a transistor.","back":"β = I_C / I_B. It can be as high as 500."},{"front":"Write β in terms of α.","back":"β = α / (1 − α)."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":5}],"figures":[{"id":"m2-pn-bias","src":"m2/pages/p14.png","crop":null,"caption":"Forward bias: positive battery terminal to P, negative to N, so the carriers are pushed towards the junction and cross it. Reverse bias is the opposite connection and widens the depletion region.","alt":"Two PN junctions, one forward biased and one reverse biased, showing the depletion region and battery polarity."},{"id":"m2-diode-vi","src":"m2/pages/p17.png","crop":null,"caption":"Diode V–I curve: the knee at 0.3 V (germanium) or 0.7 V (silicon), tiny reverse leakage, then breakdown.","alt":"Graph of diode current against voltage with forward and reverse regions and the breakdown point."},{"id":"m2-rectifiers","src":"m2/pages/p19.png","crop":null,"caption":"Half-wave rectifier with one diode, and full-wave bridge rectifier with four diodes, with input and output waveforms.","alt":"Half-wave and full-wave rectifier circuits with their waveforms."},{"id":"m2-zener-vi","src":"m2/pages/p21.png","crop":null,"caption":"Zener V–I curve: in reverse breakdown the voltage stays near V_Z while the current moves between I_Z(min) and I_Z(max).","alt":"Zener diode characteristic with the constant voltage breakdown region."},{"id":"m2-zener-reg","src":"m2/pages/p23.png","crop":null,"caption":"Zener voltage regulator: series resistor R, Zener in parallel with the load R_L, and a steady output despite a varying input.","alt":"Circuit of a Zener diode regulator with load and input and output voltage graph."},{"id":"m2-bjt-symbols","src":"m2/pages/p30.png","crop":null,"caption":"NPN and PNP symbols. The arrow is on the emitter.","alt":"Symbols of NPN and PNP transistors with labelled terminals."},{"id":"m2-bjt-currents","src":"m2/pages/p33.png","crop":null,"caption":"Currents and voltages of a biased NPN and PNP transistor: I_E = I_B + I_C.","alt":"NPN and PNP transistors with I_E, I_B, I_C and bias voltages marked."},{"id":"m2-bjt-config","src":"m2/pages/p38.png","crop":null,"caption":"Common-base, common-emitter and common-collector connections.","alt":"Three transistor circuits with the common terminal grounded."},{"id":"m2-ce-input","src":"m2/pages/p40.png","crop":null,"caption":"CE input characteristic: I_B against V_BE for V_CE = 0, 10 and 20 V.","alt":"Family of input curves of a common-emitter transistor."},{"id":"m2-ce-output","src":"m2/pages/p41.png","crop":null,"caption":"CE output characteristic: I_C against V_CE for I_B from 0 to 60 µA, with the active, saturation and cut-off regions.","alt":"Family of output curves of a common-emitter transistor with three regions."}]});
